                  "type": "quiz",
                  "question": "Effective February 16, 2026, what major change does the CARES Act § 3221 Final Rule make to 42 CFR Part 2 SUD records?",
                  "options": [
                    {
                      "text": "Part 2 is repealed entirely",
                      "correct": false
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Patients must consent to each individual TPO disclosure as before",
                      "correct": false
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A single patient consent now permits all future TPO disclosures, and HITECH breach notification rules apply to Part 2 records",
                      "correct": true
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Court orders are no longer needed for any disclosure",
                      "correct": false
                    }
                  ],
                  "explanation": "The CARES Act § 3221 (March 2020) directed HHS to align 42 CFR Part 2 with HIPAA for TPO purposes. The implementing Final Rule (89 FR 12472, effective April 16, 2024, compliance February 16, 2026) makes the following key changes: (1) a SINGLE patient consent now covers all future TPO disclosures (revocable in writing) — replacing the prior per-disclosure model that frustrated EHR integration; (2) CMP tiers harmonized with HIPAA; (3) HITECH Breach Notification Rule now applies to Part 2 records; (4) patients have a right to accounting of disclosures for 3 years. The redisclosure prohibition notice STILL applies, criminal-use prohibition without court order STILL applies, and court orders are STILL required for non-TPO compelled disclosures. Part 2 was not repealed — it was modernized.",
